1 Introduction
**************

Wordwise annotates _uncommon_ words in reading-oriented buffers with
one-line dictionary definitions.  The pieces:

   • A bundled frequency list of roughly 9,500 common English words,
     divided into five tiers, decides which words are common enough to
     need no explanation.

   • A difficulty level from 1 to 5 (*note Difficulty Levels::) controls
     how aggressively words are annotated.

   • Definitions come from <https://dict.org> over the DICT protocol,
     fetched on one shared asynchronous connection so that typing and
     scrolling never wait on the network.

   • Results are cached, and the cache can persist across Emacs restarts
     through ‘savehist’ (*note Caching and persistence::).

   • Annotation is restricted to prose: source blocks, inline code,
     links, drawers, and code-like identifiers are skipped, and the mode
     only activates in reading-oriented major modes (Org, Markdown, nov,
     eww by default).

   The name and the interaction pattern are an homage to Amazon Kindle's
Word Wise feature; this package is not affiliated with Amazon.


