* The DICT client::             One asynchronous connection.
* Caching and persistence::     At most one lookup per word, ever.


File: wordwise.info,  Node: Prose detection,  Next: The DICT client,  Up: How It Works

6.1 Prose detection
===================

Only words of four or more letters in prose regions are considered.
Wordwise decides "prose" by the face at each word's position: text
fontified as Org source or example blocks, Markdown code blocks, inline
code or verbatim, drawers, property lines, links, tags, or keywords is
skipped.  Org quote, verse, and center blocks hold prose and are
annotated like ordinary paragraphs.  Because the scroll hook can reach
text before font-lock has fontified it, Wordwise fontifies the region
first (in buffers that use font-lock) so a source block never
masquerades as prose.

   Words that look like code identifiers are also skipped regardless of
face: camelCase, ALL-CAPS, and anything containing an underscore or
slash.


File: wordwise.info,  Node: The DICT client,  Next: Caching and persistence,  Prev: Prose detection,  Up: How It Works

6.2 The DICT client
===================

Definitions come from ‘dict.org’, TCP port 2628, over the DICT protocol.
Wordwise keeps at most one connection open and sends one ‘DEFINE’
request at a time; the first sense of the first returned definition
becomes the hint, truncated to ‘wordwise-max-hint-length’.

   Everything is asynchronous.  A request that exceeds
‘wordwise-request-timeout’ is treated as failed; a connection failure
backs off for 60 seconds before retrying, and word lookups that failed
are retried automatically once the cooldown expires.  Failures never
block redisplay and never error into your editing.


File: wordwise.info,  Node: Caching and persistence,  Prev: The DICT client,  Up: How It Works

6.3 Caching and persistence
===========================

Every answer is cached in the hash table ‘wordwise-cache’: both found
definitions and confirmed "no such word" responses (DICT status 552).  A
word therefore costs at most one network round-trip, ever.  Connection
failures and timeouts are _not_ cached persistently -- they are retried
after a short cooldown in the same session.

   If you enable ‘savehist-mode’, Wordwise registers the cache with
‘savehist-additional-variables’, so definitions persist across Emacs
restarts in your ‘history’ file.  Note that the registration happens
when the wordwise library loads; if you defer loading (for example with
‘use-package’'s ‘:bind’) and want the persisted cache preserved even in
sessions where you never toggle the mode, add the registration eagerly
in your init file:

     (with-eval-after-load 'savehist
       (add-to-list 'savehist-additional-variables 'wordwise-cache))

   ‘M-x wordwise-clear-cache’ flushes everything explicitly.


File: wordwise.info,  Node: Privacy,  Next: Troubleshooting,  Prev: How It Works,  Up: Top

7 Privacy
*********

Every word that Wordwise looks up is sent to ‘dict.org’ in plain text
over an unencrypted TCP connection.  The words come from the buffers you
enable the mode in, filtered to uncommon vocabulary.  Do not enable
‘wordwise-mode’ in buffers whose vocabulary you consider sensitive, and
be aware that a network observer could see the looked-up words.

   Nothing else leaves your machine: the frequency list is bundled, and
the cache is stored locally.


File: wordwise.info,  Node: Troubleshooting,  Next: Index,  Prev: Privacy,  Up: Top

8 Troubleshooting
*****************

"Wordwise not available in MODE"
     The current major mode is not in ‘wordwise-allowed-modes’.  Add it
     if you really want annotations there.

No hints appear at all
     Check that the words on screen are actually uncommon at your
     difficulty (*note Difficulty Levels::) -- at the default level,
     everything in the top ~5,000 English words is silently skipped.
     Then check the echo area for "Wordwise DICT unavailable": without a
     network path to dict.org port 2628, only previously cached words
     can be annotated.  Wordwise retries the connection every 60
     seconds.

Hints appear on code
     The skip list is face-based (see ‘wordwise--skip-faces’); a mode
     that fontifies code with faces Wordwise does not know about will
     leak through.  Report the mode and face, or add the face to the
     list.

Hints are stale or wrong
     Definitions are cached indefinitely, including "no definition
     found".  ‘M-x wordwise-clear-cache’ refetches everything.


